illion  windmills is a lot of windmills and one  of these that you might have recognized  it's just trying to locate 11 windmill 1  windmill farm along the coast in this  region caused enormous fewer well one  windmill farm is going to be nothing  compared to the number of windmill farms  we're going to need to provide the kind  of energy that we currently get from  fossil fuels so one of these you have to  recognize is that fossil fuels are bad  and I totally agree with that aspect and  they're bad in more than just the fact  that they release carbon but one of  these you also have to recognize is that  some of the alternatives that were  considering to replace the fossil fuels  also have serious problems so in  conclusion I was hoping that I could  encourage the sturm report to finish  that is to go back and look at six-fifty  look at seven fifty parts per million  actually do a careful analysis of why  550 is better than 650 given their  assumptions come back justify why they  think this five percent of GDP is just a  good ideas as a reasonable estimate of  what kind of damages we actually were  going to get and then do a better job of  explaining the difference between  moderate abatement and dramatic  near-term abatement because that's  really where the policy issue is that  the idea that we're not going to do any  abatement I think is going to be an  issue that's going to disappear in a  couple years when we have a change in  there will be a Bateman but the question  is whether we need dramatic near-term  strong abatement with very high costs or  whether we should start with moderate  abatement that's the place where the  debate needs to go thank you taking  thanks to both them to Gary and Rob the  comments the lot of what Gary said was I  think looking at what we've done  expressing it in a somewhat different  way but in the same kind of spirit that  we offered the so let me just pick up on  a couple of a couple of points there and  again I think Gary like Ro