ing 700 pages is people  don't always read it all and if you and  that's our fault I apologize for that  but we do in chapter 13 have a fairly  detailed discussion of the way in which  these kinds of calculations of damages  have to be put together and the argument  that we were making was a strategic  argument using the model which was  similar to the strategic argument I  started off with that when we're talking  about stabilization levels we should be  thinking of something at or below 550  parts per million we ran that in terms  of risk and we ran that in terms of the  model so it's not saying 550 or nothing  it's saying here are very good arguments  for coming below 550 where below 550 we  don't know we heard the discussion this  morning that 550 is outrageously high  that's one point of view we need to have  that discussion 450 is probably very  very hard to do so somewhere between 450  and 550 so we used both the intuitive  risk reduction approach and the modeling  approach to say we should we be below  550 and we left open we're below 5 15 as  we heard this morning  that is a really important discussion to  have if you really think we should wait  and see and invest their money in nice  high rate of return projects I give the  same answer like a really a few minutes  ago to to Bill I mean you're taking  yourself into difficult territory in my  view you'd be running up against a price  of environment relative to other goods  which is going up very quickly and that  rate of return in terms of what it buys  for environment reduction would not be  high enough to offset the rising price  in environmental goods so you going to  be very careful to treat this as  specifically the many good model it is  and not to ramp it down to one good  model or reduce it to one good model  just by assuming effectively constant  prices on the cheapness of mitigation  that is an area where it does seem to me  we have to look much more closely I'd  simply remarked that the Internati